{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Only take heed to yourself, and guard your soul greatly, lest you forget the things.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: When will you be called wise and understanding? When you do not forget them and treat them according to their truth; for if you distort them out of forgetfulness, you will be accounted fools — end of quote. It appears from his language that in his view the word רק (only) refers back to what precedes, meaning: When will the nations say of them, ״רק עם חכם ונבון״ — 'a wise and understanding people only' (Deuteronomy 4:6)? When you do not forget them; for if not so, there would have been no need to mention the word רק, but rather the verse should have begun, ״השמר לך״." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret the word רק as follows: this expression of diminishment — that the nations are diminished and lacking compared to your stature — is only because of the Torah you received at Mount Sinai; and since that is so, the verse says, ״Take heed to yourself and guard your soul greatly, lest you forget״ the standing at Mount Sinai. And the phrase ״the things which your eyes have seen״ is like the expression used at Sinai (Exodus 20:15): ״וכל העם רואים את הקולות ואת הלפידים״ — 'and all the people saw the voices and the torches' — for if he forgets and removes the matter of that exalted and wondrous standing from his heart, he will come to deny a fundamental principle, and this is the destruction of body and soul, and this is the sense of ״take heed to yourself and guard your soul greatly.״ And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״השמר לך פן תשכח״ — a warning that a person should not engage in things that would cause him to forget the Torah, and that he should not remove it from his heart. And this is what they said in tractate Avot: 'Whoever forgets a thing from his Mishnah — Scripture accounts it as if he were liable with his soul, as it is said, השמר לך ושמור נפשך מאד. I might think even if his Mishnah overwhelmed him (i.e., he forgot under duress) — Scripture says, ופן יסורו מלבבך כל ימי חייך; thus he is not liable unless he removes them from his heart.' And they further interpreted in another place: 'Whoever forgets one thing from his learning transgresses three prohibitions (לאוין), as it is said, השמר לך ושמור נפשך מאד פן תשכח; and wherever Scripture says השמר, פן, or אל, it is nothing other than a negative commandment (לא תעשה).'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:23.464727210Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}